Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be giants or dwarfs depending on distance; less directly indicative alone.
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means greater distance; giants tend to be luminous and can be seen at larger distances with smaller parallax.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty; less relevant for classification but large errors reduce confidence.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral class; giants often have specific B-V ranges depending on spectral type.
- SpType (spectral type): Directly encodes luminosity class (V = main sequence/dwarf, III = giant, II = bright giant, Ib = supergiant), so it is the most important feature.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Intrinsic brightness; giants generally have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs.

Step 2. Based on the above examples and Step 1's results, infer 10 different conditions per answer:

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant stars):
- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, F0V, F5V, F6V, A1V, F5/F6V]
- SpType is in categories containing "V" only (main sequence stars)
- Amag >= 17
- Plx >= 4
- B-V <= 0.6
- Vmag >= 7.5
- Amag is within range of [17, 19]
- Plx is within range of [5, 20]
- B-V is within range of [0.3, 0.6]
- SpType does not contain "III" or "II" or "Ib" (no giant or supergiant classes)

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant stars):
- SpType is in categories containing "III" or "II" or "Ib" (e.g., K3III, B8II/III, B1Ib/II)
- Amag < 17
- Plx < 4
- B-V > 0.8
- Vmag < 8.5
- Amag is within range of [13, 16.5]
- Plx is within range of [0.8, 4]
- B-V is within range of [1.0, 1.7]
- SpType is in [K3III, K5/M0III, K3/K4III, G8III, K0III, K2III:]
- SpType contains "III" or "II" or "Ib" or similar luminosity classes indicating giants

These conditions align with the examples and known stellar classification principles.

