Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs; alone it is not a strong indicator because apparent brightness depends on distance.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; giants tend to be luminous but can be far away with small parallax, while dwarfs are often closer with larger parallax.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement error; large error reduces reliability of parallax but does not directly indicate giant/dwarf.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral class; giants and dwarfs can have overlapping B-V, but very red (high B-V) stars are often giants.
- SpType (spectral type): The spectral type suffix (e.g., V, III, II) indicates luminosity class; "V" means dwarf (main sequence), "III" means giant, "II" bright giant, "Ib" supergiant.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Intrinsic brightness; giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs of same spectral type.

